  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1143 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nThis Afternoon and Tonight\n\nA 500 mb shortwave trough will move east northeast through the \narea this afternoon. As this occurs, mid- and high clouds will\nmove through the region. With steep lapse rates above 450 mb,\nthere might be some high virga. High temperatures will range\nfrom the mid-40s to lower 50s.\n\nIn the wake of this shortwave tonight, a 500 mb ridge will\nquickly build across the Upper Mississippi River Valley. Skies\nwill gradually clear and the moon will set around 11 pm. This\nshould provide favorable conditions to see the peak of the\nLeonid meteor shower (hourly rates around 15) overnight tonight.\nLow temperatures will be in the 30s. However, a southerly wind\nof 5 to 15 mph will make it feel like the lower and mid-30s.\n\nTuesday through Wednesday\n\nA 500 mb ridge axis will slide just east over the area on\nTuesday and remain that way into Wednesday. Southerly wind at\nthe surface and aloft will bring unseasonable warmth northward \ninto the region. High temperatures will be in the mid- and upper\n50s (normals are in the lower and mid-40s) and low temperatures\nwill range from the mid-30s to lower 40s (normals are in the\nmid- and upper 20s).\nRain totals will range from a quarter to 1.5\ninches of rain. The highest rain totals will be in southeast\nMinnesota and north-central Wisconsin.\nWednesday night\n\nA 500 mb shortwave trough will eject out of the Northern and \nCentral Plains and move northeast through the Upper Mississippi\nRiver Valley. Soundings show that there will be some saturation\nbetween 850 and 600 mb. This may result in some isolated\nshowers. The highest probabilities (up to 20 percent) will be in\nsoutheast Minnesota. Low temperatures will be in the mid- to\nupper 40s.\n\nThursday through Friday\n\nA 500 mb ridge will build across the area on Thursday and remain\nin control of the weather through Friday. Southerly winds will\ncontinue to bring unseasonable warmth into the region. High\ntemperatures will be around 60. Low temperatures will range from\nthe mid-30s to lower 40s.\n\nThis weekend\n\nA 500 mb longwave trough will move east through the region on\nSaturday night. A surface low will move northeast through\nwestern and northern Minnesota on Saturday afternoon and night.\nAs this occurs, a cold front will produce rain as it moves east\nthrough the region. Rain totals will range from a quarter to 1.5\ninches of rain. The highest rain totals will be in southeast\nMinnesota and north-central Wisconsin.\n\nHigh temperatures on Saturday will range from the mid-50s to \nlower 60s. Low temperatures on Saturday night will be in the\nlower and mid-30s. High temperatures will be more seasonable on\nSunday ranging from the mid-30s to lower 40s.",
